# Known differences for a MariaDB against MySQL run:
#   KNOWN_FILE=corlogic.known.mysql
# The line forms are the ones corlogic.known documents. Every entry below is a place where
# the two products promise different things, so a difference there is the dialect and not a
# defect. Nothing here is muted on a same-vendor run.
#
# A function only one of them has, and the same function used where the other cannot take
# it. GROUPING() is the one this turns up most.
ERROR|1305v0|*|*
ERROR|0v1305|*|*
ERROR|1221v0|*|*
ERROR|0v1221|*|*

# QUICK is a MariaDB keyword in DELETE. MySQL reads it as a table alias, so the same text
# is a different statement there and the WHERE clause compares another column.
ERROR|*|*|DELETE QUICK*

# An unused common table expression: MariaDB resolves its column names and MySQL does not,
# so a CTE that names a column it does not have is an error on one side only.
ERROR|1054v0|*CTE*|*
ERROR|0v1054|*CTE*|*

# MySQL refuses a subquery that reads the table the statement updates or deletes from.
ERROR|0v1093|*|*
ERROR|1093v0|*|*

# `utf8` is an alias for utf8mb3 on MySQL and for utf8mb4 on a recent MariaDB, so a
# statement that mixes an introducer literal with a binary literal aggregates to a
# different column type on each side. Measured against CS 13.1.0: MySQL 5.5, 5.6 and 5.7
# differ, 8.0.36 and 9.1.0 agree. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
RESULT|coltype|*CSINTRO*|*

# Hexadecimal and binary string literals. MariaDB deliberately separates the two
# syntaxes: X'value' / x'value' is the SQL Standard binary string literal and stays a
# string in every context, while 0xvalue is the MySQL/MariaDB hex hybrid that acts as a
# number in a numeric context. MySQL treats both as numbers. Closed as expected behaviour
# three times - MDEV-6092, MDEV-10479 and MDEV-38434. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
RESULT|content|*|*CONV(*
RESULT|content|*|*x'*

# GREATEST and LEAST given both a string and a number. MariaDB compares the arguments as
# numbers and MySQL compares them as strings, so each picks a different argument. The two
# entries name the two statements that were judged, because the same conversion carries a
# filed bug and a wider entry would mute that as well. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
f05f2e49fd1f//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT GREATEST(cX,cX,N) AS r0 FROM tX WHERE ABS(cX) <N
58b12e6fc964//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT LEAST(cX,cX,N) AS r0 FROM t WHERE ABS(cX) <N

# WEIGHT_STRING() on a temporal value. MariaDB returns the bytes of the string form and
# MySQL returns the packed sort key. Over a string column both return collation weights, so
# only a temporal argument differs. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
424985bb422a//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT WEIGHT_STRING(cX) AS r0 FROM tX

# COMPRESSION= as a table option. MySQL carries it for InnoDB page compression and takes
# the statement, MariaDB has no such option and answers ER_UNKNOWN_OPTION. MariaDB asks for
# page compression through PAGE_COMPRESSED instead. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
ERROR|0v1911|*|*COMPRESSION=*
ERROR|1911v0|*|*COMPRESSION=*

# A temporary table named twice in one statement: MySQL refuses it with
# ER_CANT_REOPEN_TABLE, MariaDB opens it again.
ERROR|0v1137|*|*
ERROR|1137v0|*|*

# ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY: MySQL reads functional dependence and checks ORDER BY against the
# GROUP BY its own way, so each vendor refuses statements the other takes.
ERROR|0v1055|*|*
ERROR|1055v0|*|*

# A string compared with a DATE value that is not a date: MySQL fails the statement with
# ER_WRONG_VALUE, MariaDB warns and compares.
ERROR|0v1525|*|*
ERROR|1525v0|*|*

# MySQL refuses a JSON function argument that is not valid JSON with ER_INVALID_JSON_TEXT_IN_PARAM;
# MariaDB returns NULL with a warning.
ERROR|0v3141|*|*
ERROR|3141v0|*|*

# A number outside the TIME range: MariaDB clamps it to 838:59:59 with a warning, MySQL returns NULL.
RESULT|content|*CAST*|*CAST(N AS TIME*

# ORDER BY a plain column in a query with an aggregate and no GROUP BY: MariaDB refuses it with
# ER_MIX_OF_GROUP_FUNC_AND_FIELDS in every sql_mode, MySQL returns the one row.
ERROR|1140v0|*ORDERBY*|*
ERROR|0v1140|*ORDERBY*|*

# GREATEST and LEAST over a string and a number: MariaDB compares them as numbers, MySQL as
# strings, so the two return different values.
RESULT|content|*|*GREATEST(*
RESULT|content|*|*LEAST(*

# CAST of an out-of-range DOUBLE expression to an integer: MariaDB clamps it with a warning,
# MySQL fails the statement with ER_DATA_OUT_OF_RANGE.
ERROR|0v1690|*CAST*|*AS SIGNED*
ERROR|0v1690|*CAST*|*AS UNSIGNED*

# An SRID that MySQL does not hold in its spatial reference system catalog: MySQL fails with
# ER_SRS_NOT_FOUND, MariaDB takes any SRID.
ERROR|0v3548|*|*

# A value outside the range of the target type, reached through any expression: MariaDB clamps it
# and warns, MySQL fails the statement with ER_DATA_OUT_OF_RANGE.
ERROR|0v1690|*|*

# A foreign key whose referenced column has no index: MariaDB creates the index, MySQL refuses the
# constraint with ER_FK_NO_INDEX_PARENT.
ERROR|0v1822|*|*

# A string compared with a number in a DELETE or an UPDATE: MariaDB warns and carries on, MySQL
# stops the statement with ER_TRUNCATED_WRONG_VALUE.
ERROR|0v1292|*DELETE*|*
ERROR|0v1292|*UPDATE*|*

# MySQL has a JSON column type and refuses a JSON function over a plain string with
# ER_INVALID_TYPE_FOR_JSON. MariaDB stores JSON as text, so the same call succeeds.
ERROR|0v3146|*|*

# The column width of a recursive CTE comes from its first branch in MariaDB, so a growing value
# hits ER_DATA_TOO_LONG. MySQL widens a JSON column, and a plain string grows the same way in
# both. As described in MDEV-29318.
ERROR|1406v0|*CTE*|*
